      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To evaluate the effects of pharmacist-conducted medication therapy review (MTR) and intervention on the quality of care of patients in a family medicine clinic."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "DESIGN",
          "content": "Prospective, observational, cohort study."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "SETTING",
          "content": "Family medicine clinic in Minnesota during 2000-2001."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "PATIENTS",
          "content": "Patients were enrolled in a statewide nonprofit managed care organization; selected patients were seen by a clinical pharmacist."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "INTERVENTION",
          "content": "Following MTR, medication-related problems (MRPs) were identified and resolved."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "MAIN OUTCOME MEASURES",
          "content": "MRPs identified and resolved, improvement in clinical status, achievement of therapeutic goals, important medication use, and reduction in number of medications."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "92 patients were included in the study, with a total of 203 patient encounters. MRPs were identified in 90% of patients, with a total of 250 identified. Overall status of medical conditions improved in 45% of patients, 46% stayed the same, and 9% declined (P < 0.001). Significant improvement in status was found for hypertension (P = 0.007), dyslipidemia (P = 0.002), and asthma (P = 0.011). Significant improvement was seen for aspirin use for myocardial infarction prevention (50% vs. 93%, P = 0.031) and inhaled steroids for asthma (36% vs. 64%, P = 0.031). The number of medications was reduced from an average of 3.92 to 3.04 (P < 0.001) per patient."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "MTR and intervention by a pharmacist positively affected quality of care in this family medicine clinic."
        }
      ]},
